// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row vec_of_mutref_elem_index_picks_index_not_index_mut_refuses (tier 3).
// LEGAL PROGRAM REFUSED. `vs[0u64].push(&c)` with `vs: Vec<&mut Vec<&C>>` is legal
// Rust; rustc's only complaint about this program is E0502 at `c.bump()`, which
// logosc now also gives. MEASURED with rustc 1.98.1 by path, 2026-09-22.
// TODAY logosc refuses the push instead, saying a place is written behind a `&`
// reference — and it is RIGHT about what it was handed: indexing lowered to the
// SHARED `Vec::index`, giving `&&mut Vec<&C>`, and no `&mut` can be taken out of
// a `&`. The defect is the METHOD CHOICE. Rust selects `IndexMut` when the
// indexed place is the receiver of a `&mut self` method; sema here does not, so
// the borrow checker is answering a question about a program nobody wrote.
// The message also prints the temporary's internal name.
//
// SUPERSEDES the `..._receiver_store_loan_unheld_admits` row (issue #254): that
// row recorded the opposite defect — this program ADMITTED — and the ADR 0028
// checker closed it. What is left is this over-refusal, which is a different
// defect in a different subsystem and gets its own row rather than a silent
// close.

package vec_of_mutref_elem_receiver_store_loan_unheld_admits;
struct C { n: i64 }
impl C { fn bump(self: &mut C) { self.n = self.n + 1i64; } }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut c: C = C { n: 1i64 };
    let mut inner: Vec<&C> = Vec::new();
    {
        let mut vs: Vec<&mut Vec<&C>> = Vec::new();
        vs.push(&mut inner);
        vs[0u64].push(&c);
    }
    c.bump();
    return inner.len() as i32;
}
// rustc 1.98.1 --edition 2024 (round 2026-09-15f-rustcaudit, class CONFIRMED): REFUSED — error[E0502]: cannot borrow `c` as mutable because it is also borrowed as immutable. Twin src/compiler/probes/2026-09-15f-rustcaudit/rust/vec_of_mutref_elem_receiver_store_loan_unheld_admits.rs. Audit note: c auto-Copy under A16 is not leaned on (c only borrowed).
